# Docstrings (Google style)

Follow the [Google Python Style Guide — Docstrings](https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#38-comments-and-docstrings).

## Format

- Use `"""` triple-double-quotes (PEP 257).
- One-line summary (≤80 chars when practical), ending with `.`, `?`, or `!`.
- For longer docstrings: blank line after the summary, then body at the same indent as the opening `"""`.
- Prefer **imperative mood** for functions and methods (`Fetch rows.`, not `Fetches rows.`).
- `@property` docstrings describe the attribute (`The workbook path.`), not `Returns the …`.

## Sections

Use these headings (each ends with `:`). Omit sections that add no information beyond the signature and type hints.

| Section | When |
|---------|------|
| `Args:` | Non-obvious parameters (types come from annotations when clear) |
| `Returns:` | Non-`None` return semantics not obvious from the summary |
| `Yields:` | Generators |
| `Raises:` | Interface-relevant exceptions |
| `Attributes:` | Public class attributes (not `@property`) |
| `Examples:` | Non-obvious usage |

Hang section content with **4 spaces** (match surrounding file).

## References

- Mark inline code and symbol names with **single backticks**: `DependencyGraph`,
  `excel_grapher.runtime.lookup.xl_index`, `Sheet1!A1`.
- Use ASCII `->` instead of Unicode arrows (`→`) so doc extraction works on all platforms.

## Scope

- **Public API** (`__all__`, exported names): docstrings required for non-trivial functions, classes, and modules.
- **Test modules**: module docstrings only when they document unusual setup, env deps, or run instructions — not `"""Tests for foo."""`.
- **`@override` methods**: omit docstrings unless behavior materially differs from the base; then document only the delta.

## Generated code

Series-binding codegen defaults to the `google` docstring renderer (`GoogleSeriesDocstringRenderer`).
